[coal] Remove Coal's debug printers, which require Eigen's IO

Coal's disp() methods stream Eigen expressions and build Eigen::IOFormat
objects. Eigen supports being configured with EIGEN_NO_IO, which omits
Eigen/src/Core/IO.h entirely; under that configuration these headers do not
compile, so no consumer who sets EIGEN_NO_IO can include collision_data.h or
math/transform.h at all.

Drake is such a consumer: it sets -DEIGEN_NO_IO=1 project-wide and formats
Eigen objects with drake::fmt_eigen instead (see drake/common/fmt_eigen.h), so
every Drake translation unit that includes any Coal header hits this.

Rather than guard the individual streaming statements, this patch #if 0's out
each disp() together with the operator<<() that forwards to it. Two reasons:

 - The printers exist to back __str__ and __repr__ on Coal's Python bindings
   (python/printable.hh binds them via PrintableVisitor) and to print state in
   Coal's C++ tests. Drake builds neither python/ nor test/ -- see
   package.BUILD.bazel, which globs only include/ and src/ -- and nothing in
   include/ or src/ calls disp() or streams these types. So the whole set is
   dead code in Drake's build.

 - The printers call each other, so guarding only the seven that themselves
   touch Eigen IO (Contact, QueryRequest, QueryResult, CollisionResult,
   ContactPatch and DistanceResult in collision_data.h, plus Transform3s)
   would leave three live callers of now-removed printers:
   CollisionRequest::disp and DistanceRequest::disp forward to
   QueryRequest::disp, and ContactPatchResult::disp forwards to
   ContactPatch::disp. The guards have to cover that closure anyway, which is
   all eleven. Taking the whole set is self-consistent and does not need
   revisiting each time upstream streams one more Eigen expression.

Reasoning for not upstreaming this patch as written: it is a hack that
accommodates Drake's usage (we don't build the python bindings). But it is not
a general solution for Coal.

Guidance if someone picks this up for upstream. The fix is small, because
EIGEN_NO_IO gates only two things in Eigen/Core: the <iosfwd> include and
src/Core/IO.h. What that removes is the definition of IOFormat (the forward
declaration in ForwardDeclarations.h survives), WithFormat,
operator<<(ostream&, const DenseBase&), and in practice DenseBase::format(),
which is still declared in DenseBase.h but returns the now-incomplete
WithFormat. Coefficient access -- rows(), cols(), coeff() -- is untouched, so
Coal can format matrices itself:

  - Add a stream wrapper, say coal::internal::eigen_str(expr), in a new
    coal/math/eigen_io.h. Its operator<< delegates to Eigen under a normal
    build and walks coefficients under EIGEN_NO_IO, emitting the same
    "[a, b, c]" shape Eigen's default produces.

  - Route the fifteen streamed Eigen expressions in the six affected disp()
    methods of collision_data.h through it. No call site then needs an #ifdef.

  - Transform3s::disp is the one caller that wants custom formatting, since it
    builds its row separators out of `prefix`. Either give Coal its own
    analogue of IOFormat so the output is byte-identical either way, or keep
    the IOFormat path when Eigen IO is present and fall back to the generic
    shape when it is not. The latter is one #ifdef in one function and still
    prints the transform.

That version would supersede this patch entirely.

